## Method and reasoning
Each tree entry stores a block sum. Build the answer index one bit at a time from high to low. If the next candidate block contains fewer than k remaining copies, skip it and subtract its block sum from k. The final index is the largest prefix with fewer than the original k copies. The next value is the answer, unless the index reached n.

## Walk through the sample
Frequencies [0,2,0,1,0] have prefixes [0,2,2,3,3]. Rank 2 first appears at index 2, rank 3 at index 4. After adding five copies at index 1, rank 4 already falls in the first bucket.

## Complexity
Time O(log n) per operation, space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, q;
    cin >> n >> q;
    vector<long long> tree(n + 1);
    auto add = [&](int i, long long d) {
        for (; i <= n; i += i & -i)
            tree[i] += d;
    };
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        long long f;
        cin >> f;
        add(i, f);
    }
    int high = bit_floor(unsigned(n));
    while (q--) {
        string op;
        cin >> op;
        if (op == "ADD") {
            int i;
            long long d;
            cin >> i >> d;
            add(i, d);
        } else {
            long long rank;
            cin >> rank;
            int before = 0;
            for (int step = high; step; step /= 2)
                if (before + step <= n && tree[before + step] < rank) {
                    before += step;
                    rank -= tree[before];
                }
            cout << (before == n ? 0 : before + 1) << '\n';
        }
    }
}
~~~
